GAlgorithmChaining
==================

This example demonstrates how to *chain* several optimization algorithms in a
single optimization run.

Algorithms are appended to the Go2 object with the &-operator:

    go & "ea" & "cgd";

Go2 then executes them in sequence, handing the best individual(s) found by one
algorithm to the next. Each algorithm runs its own full iteration budget (as
configured in its JSON file under ./config/), starting its iteration counter at
zero -- a chained algorithm does NOT inherit the previous algorithm's final
iteration number.

The chosen chain is a common pattern: an evolutionary algorithm ("ea") performs
the global exploration first, then conjugate gradient descent ("cgd") locally refines its
best result.

The optimized problem (a GFunctionIndividual) and its factory are reused from
the Geneva library, so no problem-specific individual or factory has to be
written for this example. Edit ./config/GFunctionIndividual.json to choose the
target function and its dimensionality, and the per-algorithm JSON files to
tune each stage of the chain.

Run it with, e.g.:

    ./GAlgorithmChaining

Other algorithm mnemonics (sa, swarm, nm, cgd, ps) can be chained in the same
way, both here in code and on the command line via --optimizationAlgorithms.
